  "authority": "United Nations Conference on the Standardization of Geographical Names, resolution XI/3 (2017); based on the Beirut 2007 Unified Arabic Transliteration System, amended Beirut 2008 and Riyadh 2017",
  "description": "UN-recommended romanization for geographical names in Arabic-speaking countries that adopt it (partly implemented in Jordan, Oman, Saudi Arabia). Uses macron below where BGN/PCGN uses cedilla: ẖ = ح, s̱ = ص, ḏ = ض, ṯ = ط, d͟h = ظ; the article is assimilated and always capitalised (Ash Shāriqah).",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_ar.pdf",
    "https://unstats.un.org/unsd/geoinfo/ungegn/docs/11th-uncsgn-docs/E_Conf.105_137_CRP.137_Annex%201_Arabic%20Romanization%20system.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Arabic"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Glyph collision: in this system ẖ is ح, ḏ is ض and ṯ is ط, whereas in DIN/ISO ṯ is ث and ḏ is ذ (and ẖ is خ in ISO 233).",
    "The article is written with a capital initial in every position and separated by a space (Al Balad, Minyat Aḏ Ḏinniyyah); hamza (’) is not romanized word-initially; ة is h (the construct-state t is not generated); nunation is ignored; a middle dot separates s·h, d·h, k·h, t·h.",
    "-iyy- is kept doubled (Ḏinniyyah). Final -iyy is written ī by analogy with BGN/PCGN (not stated in the UNGEGN summary).",
    "بن is Bin and ابن Ibn by analogy with BGN/PCGN Special Rule 5 (personal names are outside the scope of the UN system).",
    "Values from the UNGEGN WGRS report v5.0 (2018); the 2017 conference annex itself was not consulted."
  ],
